  "answer": "The opponent goes first—but this is the first opportunity to file evidence, not an unconditional requirement to rely on evidence.\n\n**Manual practice:** The opponent bears the onus of establishing its case and has the first opportunity to prepare evidence in support. Evidence in support must be filed within three months after the opponent is given a copy of the notice of intention to defend. If the opponent does not intend to rely on evidence in support, they should notify IP Australia in writing. [TMM/Part51/1/1/1]\n\n**Opposition to registration/protection — Manual practice:** If no evidence in support is filed, the applicant must file any evidence in answer to the statement of grounds and particulars within three months after the Registrar notifies them that no evidence in support was filed. [TMM/Part51/1/1/2]\n\n**Non-use removal opposition — important exception in Manual practice:** If the opponent files no evidence in support and does not ask to be heard within the evidence-in-support period, the proceedings are taken to have ended. Provided the non-use application is in order, section 97 then applies. [TMM/Part49/6#1]\n\n**Act:** Section 97(1) requires removal for the goods and/or services specified in the application where there is no opposition or the opposition has been dismissed. [TMA1995/s97]\n\nThe procedural account above comes from the Manual; the supplied Regulations extract does not independently set out the complete evidence-in-support procedure."
